## INTRODUCTION
Luminescence detection of target molecules represents a promising research field owing to its straightforward implementation, rapid response time, high sensitivity, and nakedeye detection properties.1 In particular, luminescence sensors allow convenient and flexible detection of volatile organic compounds (VOCs).2 In recent decades, extensive investigation has been performed on luminescence energy shift3 (color change) and luminescence quenching4 (light-off) detection. In comparison to these methods, luminescence enhancement from darkness (light-up) can be readily observed by the naked eyes, which remains challenging, though.5 So far, light-up sensors have been designed based on the aggregation-induced emission (AIE)6 or solvent-induced energy transfer effects,7 and they could be employed in real-time for in situ visual detection of target molecules. However, AIE detection requires the use of a solventphase,8 and solvent-induced energy transfer has limited sensitivity for light-up detection and poor recycling performances.9 These limitations significantly hinder industrial applications of these methods. Therefore, developing a new light-up method would greatly expand the scope of applications of luminescent sensors.
Different from the light-up method based on AIE or solventinduced energy transfer, low-temperature enhanced luminescence is based on the lock of molecular vibrations at low temperatures and the decrease of non-radiative decay, thus offering a different access route to light-up sensing.10 The mechanism of the lowtemperature enhanced luminescence is well known; however, application of this method is still limited because its working temperature is too low. Therefore, locking molecular vibrations at room temperature, if achieved, would significantly improve the applicability and practicability of the method.
Scheme 1. The Model of Light-Up Luminescent Detection by a Guest-Lock Process.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are custom-designed crystalline porous materials11 with outstanding performances in various fields, such as gas adsorption,12 catalysis,13 and luminescent detection.14 The abundant binding sites for weak interactions in MOFs make them suitable for the design and operation of efficient molecular sensors and switches.15 Therefore, MOFs could be good candidates for achieving the lock of molecular vibrations at room temperature, by way of weak interactions between the guest molecules and the binding sites of MOFs. Remarkably, the lability of these contacts allows rapid association/dissociation between host and guest under ambient conditions,16 which may thus lead to desired sensitivity, and specificity, and recyclability in the detection of molecular species.
Taking the above aspects into consideration, we propose a strategy that takes advantage of the weak supramolecular interactions to lock molecular vibrations and to enhance the luminescence intensity, which may allow efficient detection of specific molecules. Importantly, the precise structures of MOFs provide the opportunity to look into the mechanism of the selective luminescence detection for specific VOCs, thereby facilitating the design of luminescent sensors in more practical applications, such as the detection of toxic and environmentally hazardous VOCs like chloroalkanes, which are widely used in industry.17
Herein, we designed and synthesized a rigid $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ -based MOF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}(\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (1) $\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P}=$ tris(2-pyridyl)phosphine) with water stability and large channels. Embedding CuI moieties into the framework of 1 is expected to lead to interesting luminescence features18 and provides a potential binding environment for the chloroalkane guests. Weak host-guest interactions between the chloroalkane molecules $\mathrm{(CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}.$ ) and the MOF could decrease the molecular vibrations inside the MOF at room temperature, thus leading to a significant enhancement of luminescence (Scheme 1). This was confirmed by single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) and density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Ultrafast luminescence switching with great recyclability and excellent stability were also observed, which can be attributed to the lability of these weak interactions. This guestlock method could also be applied to the light-up detection of chlorobenzene, a common toxic and environmentally hazardous
VOC. Our work presents the first example of guest-lock induced light-up detection at room temperature, thereby paving a new avenue for the design of ultrafast light-up luminescent MOF sensors.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Preparation, Characterization, and Structural Analysis. Block-shaped single crystals of 1 were obtained by solvothermal reactions between CuI and $\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P}$ (molar ratio $\mathit{\Theta}=1\colon2$ ) in a mixed $\mathrm{CH_{3}C N/H_{2}O}$ solvent at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The phase purity of the bulk crystalline material was confirmed by comparison of the experimental powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction patterns with those generated from the single crystal diffraction data (Figures S4-S5).
The crystallographic asymmetric unit of 1 (trigonal space group $R{-}3$ ) contains half a $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ unit and one $\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P}$ ligand (Figure S6). Different from the common cubane-like, stair-step-like and planar tetra-nuclear-copper(I) structures bearing $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ moieties, the $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ unit in 1 shows a rarer rope-knot like node arrangement involving only tetracoordinated $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ ions.19 As shown in Figure 1, the symmetry-related Cu1 and Cu1A atoms are firstly connected by two doubly bridging iodides to form a $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}\right]$ subunit, and each further connects to another $\mathrm{Cu-I}$ species to form a rope-knot-like $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ unit. Such a $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ unit serves as a 4- connecting node to link four other $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ units through $\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P}$ ligands, thus affording the three-dimensional (3D) framework (Figures S7-S8). Two equivalent 3D nets are interpenetrated to form a 2-fold interpenetrating architecture (Figure S9).
Close inspection of the crystal structure of 1 along the c axis reveals the presence of hexagonal channels with wide openings, each of which is defined by six $\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P}$ ligands and six $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ units.
All the pyridyl rings are aligned along the inner walls of the channels, and thus connect with the $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}I_{4}]}$ units to form a unique binding environment for chloroalkane guest molecules. As shown in Figure 1d, the I atoms of the $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}I_{4}]}$ units can interact with the hydrogen atoms of the guest molecules, while the pyridyl rings would bind with the halogen atoms of the guest molecules through weak hydrogen bonding, thus constructing an efficient “binding pocket”. The surface area and the pore size distribution of 1 were determined by the nitrogen isotherm at $77~\mathrm{~K~}$ . As presented in Figure S10, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption results showed a typical type-I isotherm with a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $39.098~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figures S10-S11). Meanwhile, the diameter of the channel was determined as $0.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which can match with the SCXRD data (Figure S12).
Luminescent Properties. Compound 1 displays an extremely weak low-energy emission band at $580~\mathrm{nm}$ with a quantum yield (QY) of $0.03\%$ (Figure 2a) under excitation at $470\ \mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature, which corresponds to the maximum in the excitation spectrum (Figure S13). The luminescence of 1 is similar to that of other copper iodide compounds and can be assigned to a clustercentered excited state $(^{3}\mathrm{CC})$ , derived from halide-to-metal charge transfer $(\mathrm{I}\to\mathrm{Cu})$ , mixed with a $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ metal-centered transfer.20
To investigate the low temperature-induced lock behavior of the molecular vibrations in 1, temperature-dependent emissive spectra of 1 were measured at different temperatures from 300 to $80~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 2a). The results indicate that 1 exhibits luminescent thermochromism when excited at $470~\mathrm{nm}$ , and the emission peak presented a slight red shift with decreasing temperature. Upon decreasing the temperature from 300 to $80~\mathrm{K}$ , the emission peak shifts from 580 to $590~\mathrm{~nm}$ accompanied by a very large enhancement of the emission intensity (Figure S14), which can be ascribed to the decrease of thermally-induced non-radiative-decay pathways (Figure S15).21 The redshift of the emission band is due to a slight contraction of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathrm{Cu}$ distances at low temperatures.22
The vibrations-locking induced luminescent enhancement at low temperatures inspired us to examine the locking of vibrations by introducing guest molecules. The responsive behavior of 1 toward various VOCs was investigated to explore its potential for sensing. The luminescence performances of 1 after its exposing for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to various VOCs were measured and the results are presented in Figure 2b. The histogram clearly shows that most solvents give almost no enhancement or only onefold enhancement (for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})$ ; however, $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ triggers a significant fluorescence enhancement. As shown in Figure 2b, upon exposure to $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ vapor for 30 min, a strong emission band centered at $580~\mathrm{nm}$ was witnessed, indicating a remarkable enhancement of the solid-state emission of 1 after its interactions with chloroalkane vapors. However, such an increase of the luminescence intensity was not observed when $\mathrm{CCl}_{4}$ vapor was introduced into 1. Moreover, 1 did not show any color changes when exposed to various VOCs, and the related UV-vis absorption spectra did not show obvious shifts (Figure S16).
Detection Mechanism. SCXRD analysis was employed to identify the location of VOCs in 1 as well as the binding sites in the host framework. To prepare the guest molecules-loaded samples for the SCXRD test, single crystals of 1 were placed under vacuum to remove all residual guest molecules, and then exposed to VOCs for $2\mathrm{h}$ . The resultant VOC-loaded crystals were subjected to SCXRD analysis (Table S1). The SCXRD results indicate that three $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ can be found in each $\mathrm{Cu}_{12}(\mathrm{Py}_{3}\mathrm{P})_{6}\mathrm{I}_{12}$ unit, while only two guest molecules were found in the case of $\mathrm{CCl}_{4}$ (Figure S17-S21). The channel environment and the interactions between the framework 1 and the guest molecules are shown in Figure 3. The distance between the H atoms of host pyridyl rings and the guest $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is $3.17\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for H…Cl interaction and $2.18\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for H…H interaction, while the distance from wall of the host framework (I atoms) to the $\mathrm{~H~}$ atoms of the guest $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is $2.70\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . These distances indicate that there exist H…Cl, H…H and H…I weak interactions in the structure of $\mathbf{1}\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (Figure 3a).
Similar interactions also exist in the framework of $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ , (with H…I distance for $2.52\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , H…Cl distance for $2.74\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figures S20-S21). Interestingly, different from the above two molecules, $\mathrm{CCl}_{4}$ was observed to be further away from the interwall of the framework of 1, which reveals that the Cl…I interaction $(3.71\mathrm{\AA})$ is too weak in this system (Figure 3b).
To expose the crystals of 1 into the vapors of VOCs including benzene, toluene, DMF, DMA, THF, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ , and acetone, the related luminescent spectra exhibited no enhancement. Meanwhile, the location of these VOCs could not be identified owing to the high disorder of the guest VOCs in the channels through SCXRD measurements. The above SCXRD results suggested that the interactions between the MOF and these VOC molecules are so weak that the framework cannot efficiently bind these molecules, and thus did not show obvious luminescent enhancement. When exposed the crystals of 1 to the vapors of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , the luminescent density displayed a double enhancement, and the SCXRD data of $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}$ were successfully obtained. In $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , the distance between the $\mathrm{~H~}$ atoms of the host pyridyl rings and the O atoms of the guest $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ is $2.98\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for H…O interaction, and the distance from the wall of the host framework (I atoms) to the H atoms of the guest $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ is $3.11\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure S22). Similarly, in $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}$ , the distance between the H atoms of the host pyridyl rings and the O atoms of the guest $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ is $2.91\textup{\AA}$ for H…O interaction, and the distance from the wall of the host framework (I atoms) to the H atoms of the guest $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ is $3.38\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure S23). These SCXRD data revealed that the interactions between the guest $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and the MOF are relatively weaker, and thus lead to the limited luminescent enhancement (Figure 2b, left).
