The Light-Up Detection of Chlorobenzene. Recent studies have suggested that serious health concerns are associated with exposure to chlorinated hydrocarbons, especially those chloroaromatic.24 Standard detection methods in air require lengthy procedures involving absorption/desorption and the use of gas chromatography.25 Conventional detection methods for aromatic chlorinated hydrocarbons are based on GC−MS, HPLC, electrochemical systems, and colorimetric arrays, but they usually require large stationary instruments and complex components, which impede their applications in resource-limited fields.
We selected chlorobenzene as a representative sample to explore the application of 1 in the light-up detection of chloroaromatic. As shown in Figure 5, the enhancement of luminescence was successfully achieved after exposure of 1 to chlorobenzene vapor for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ . This enhancement was approximately 9-fold relative to the off-state, and the QY reached up to $16.3\%$ . After optimization, the response time was found to be $455\mathrm{~s~}$ , which compares very favorably with other reported detectors for chlorobenzene.25
Furthermore, we determined the structure of $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{PhCl}$ by singlecrystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction and determined the location of the chlorobenzene molecule within its framework (Figures S29-S30). The distance between the PhCl and the inter-wall of the framework is 2.82 Å (H…I distance). Moreover, the weak interactions between the H atoms of host pyridine rings and the guest $\mathrm{PhCl}$ were found with the distances of 2.70 Å (H…Cl) and 2.85 Å (H…H), which suggested the weak interactions stabilized the chlorobenzene molecules and locked the framework vibrations to some extent, thereby resulting in the fluorescence enhancement.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have demonstrated a guest-lock induced luminescence enhancement mechanism to realize the light-up ultrafast detection of chlorinated hydrocarbons. A novel $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}.$ 一 based MOF 1 was designed and synthesized to illuminate the above mechanism. Through introduction of chlorinated hydrocarbons to lock the molecular vibrations within MOF 1, a significant luminescence intensity enhancement could be observed. SCXRD data combined with DFT calculations shed light on the binding mechanism and the differences in luminescence when $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{CCl}_{4}$ were used as guests. Remarkably, the response time achieved for $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ was less than 1 s and the sensor displayed excellent recyclability and water stability. Furthermore, this guest-lock induced light-up mechanism has been successfully applied to the detection of chlorobenzene. This work proposes and demonstrates the utility of the guest-lock induced luminescence enhancement mechanism in molecular sensing, as well as opens up the possibility to discover high-performance ultrafast light-up luminescent sensors. Studies are underway in our laboratory by applying the guest-lock induced luminescence enhancement mechanism to explore more high-performance light-up luminescent sensors.
